AB  - Grading handwritten programming assignments for “Algorithm Design and Analysis” is burdensome and suffers from inconsistent scores. This study analyzes the potential and challenges of using large language models (LLMs) for automated grading. It proposes a technical framework based on QLoRA for parameter-efficient fine-tuning. An empirical study systematically compares three approaches: traditional machine learning, prompt learning, and fine-tuning. Experimental results show the framework's strong robustness to OCR noise. The fine-tuned model achieves an ICC of 0.8174. It significantly outperforms traditional methods and prompt learning in scoring consistency. This work provides a highly reliable automated solution for the digital transformation of teaching.
