AB  - Fine-tuning Large Language Models (LLMs) on Apple Silicon devices has gained increasing attention due to their unified memory architecture and integrated GPU capabilities. Two relevant software backends for this purpose are PyTorch with Metal Performance Shaders (MPS) and Apple’s native MLX framework. This paper presents a controlled empirical comparison of both backends for Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) fine-tuning of the TinyLlama-1.1B-Chat-v1.0 model on an Apple A18 Pro chip. Using a dataset of 200 prompt-response samples derived from the Alpaca Cleaned dataset, training was performed for 200 steps under matched experimental conditions, including a LoRA rank of eight, a LoRA scaling factor of 16, a dropout rate of 0.05, a learning rate of $\mathbf{1} \times \mathbf{1 0}^{-\mathbf{4}}$, three warmup steps, a maximum gradient norm of 1.0, and a maximum sequence length of 128 tokens. Experimental results show that MLX completes the training process in 52.15 s, compared with 4,809.89 s for MPS, corresponding to an approximately 92.2-fold speedup in training time. MLX achieves a throughput of 3.835 samples/s, whereas MPS achieves 0.0416 samples/s. Despite this substantial difference in execution time, both backends exhibit broadly similar loss behavior, with average losses of 1.626 for MLX and 1.759 for MPS, and final losses of 1.281 and 1.340, respectively. The observed results indicate that MLX provides substantially higher execution efficiency for the evaluated LoRA workload while exhibiting broadly similar loss dynamics to MPS. Gradient norms exhibit broadly similar magnitudes, although their variability differs between the two backends. These findings suggest that MLX can provide a substantial performance advantage over PyTorch MPS for LoRA fine-tuning on Apple Silicon under the evaluated experimental configuration, particularly in time-sensitive scenarios.
ER  - 

