AB  - This paper presents a parameter-efficient sentiment analysis system for low-resource, code-mixed text using LLaMA 3.1 8B Instruct fine-tuned with Quantized LowRank Adaptation (QLoRA) and 4-bit NF4 quantization. We generated a balanced synthetic dataset (2,520 samples) across entertainment, reviews, and social domains to address data scarcity. Results show $\mathbf{8 0. 2} \pm \mathbf{0. 4 \%}$ accuracy (5 runs), outperforming XLM-R $(79.6 \pm 0.3 \%$, $\mathrm{p}=0.018)$. Real-world validation: ACTSA (77.3%), DravidianCodeMix (75.8%). QLoRA enables training on single consumer GPUs ($<8 ~\text{GB}$), validating efficiency for resource-constrained NLP and framework demonstrates strong generalization capability across domains and maintains balanced precision and recall despite the presence of linguistic variability, transliteration noise, and informal expressions in Telugu-English code-mixed data. The use of synthetic data combined with parameterefficient fine-tuning significantly reduces the dependency on large annotated corpora while preserving contextual understanding. Experimental analysis also confirms that lowbit quantization and adapter-based training improve memory efficiency and scalability without degrading predictive performance. These findings highlight the practicality of deploying large language model-based sentiment systems in real-world low-resource environments, supporting future multilingual NLP applications and scalable adaptation to other under-represented languages.
