AB  - Building on previous work on parameter-efficient fine-tuning methodologies-specifically Low-Rank Adaptation approach (LoRA) proposed by Edward J. Hu et al., this paper presents an enhanced hybrid framework that combines adaptive QLoRA fine-tuning with a contrastive RAG system that significantly improves both the training efficiency and the inference quality for the code review automation domain. Although existing work done by Sumukh Naveen Aradhya et al. using LoRA, QLoRA and QLoRA with RAG showed promise in offering a good trade-off between memory utilization and output quality, issues still persist in fixed fine-tuning configurations and generic context retrieval. To address these, the proposed method adaptively adjusts the LoRA rank based on input complexity to ensure that optimal capacity is allocated to the model during fine-tuning. This paper also introduces a contrastive retrieval mechanism system that reranks semantically similar code samples based on stylistic similarity. This results in a more realistic and humanlike code review. The framework is evaluated on real-world dataset (more details on the dataset can be found in [11]) with context obtained from Meta's LLaMA 3.2 3B model. The experimental results report up to 55% savings in GPU memory, 20% improvement in CPU efficiency, and decreased entropy in inference output, outperforming prior approaches. The results establish the efficacy of adaptive QLoRA with contrastive RAG as an efficient and viable approach to efficiently automate code review generation using a fine-tuned large language model and also provides a reliable approach to efficiently fine-tune LLMs for any domain.
