AB  - The evaluation of database design quality is essential for assessing undergraduate theses (projects), yet existing approaches suffer from subjective manual reviews and limited rule representation, leading to inconsistent evaluations. This paper proposes a structured evidence-driven framework with expert preference alignment for database design quality assessment. The method constructs knowledge graphs of student and standard designs to generate structured evidence, which is combined with database design information to guide parameter-efficient fine-tuning of large language models. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed method outperforms text-only and non-fine-tuned baselines on MAE, Pearson, and Acc@1 metrics. Ablation studies further verify the effectiveness of knowledge graph enhancement and parameter-efficient fine-tuning.
