AB  - Sentiment analysis of user reviews is vital for product evaluation, yet the behavior of large language models (LLMs) in lower-resource languages such as Indonesian remains under-explored. We compare three LLM families (Gemma, Llama, Qwen) against classic RNN baselines (Bi-LSTM, Bi-GRU) on a three-class Indonesian app-review dataset (InfoBMKG, 8,000 reviews) under two settings: zero-shot and LoRA fine-tuning. In the zero-shot setting, a domain-trained Bi-LSTM outperforms all LLMs (macro-F1 0.84), indicating that general pre-training alone is insufficient for this domain. After LoRA fine-tuning, all LLMs improve substantially; Gemma3-4B-it attains an Accuracy of 0.92 and a macro-F1 score of 0.89. Paired, stratified bootstrap testing (B is 10,000; two-sided) with Holm–Bonferroni correction shows that only Gemma3-4B-it achieves a statistically significant macro-F1 gain over Bi-LSTM, while other LLMs exhibit positive but non-significant trends. Despite these gains, Bi-LSTM remains more than 70 times faster under a unified inference protocol, underscoring a practical accuracy-latency trade-off. Overall, LLMs are not uniformly superior to classic deep learning models in this setting; parameter-efficient fine-tuning is key to unlocking LLM performance for Indonesian sentiment analysis. However, deployment choices should balance accuracy targets with latency budgets.
