AB  - Consumers increasingly rely on existing reviews to guide purchasing decisions, yet face significant challenges in extracting actionable insights from vast volumes of unstructured e-commerce feedback. Automated review summarization offers a promising solution, with large language models (LLMs) emerging as powerful tools for this task. This study proposes a dual-phase LLM fine-tuning framework for review summarization, decomposing the process into two phases: (1) pros/cons extraction from individual reviews and (2) summary generation from aggregated features. Leveraging the LoRA adapter, we efficiently adapt Qwen-series LLMs for both phases, achieving domain-specific optimization with minimal computational overhead. Experimental results indicate that LLMs, including Qwen series, demonstrate robust performance in review summarization tasks on review data from Amazon. The Qwen series models adapt well to the dual-phase generation framework, with the 7-billion-parameter variant exhibiting particularly outstanding performance. In Phase I, it achieves significant performance improvements compared to smallerparameter Qwen variants and conventional deep learning models. When integrated with the purification module, this model further delivers optimal effectiveness in Phase II. Moreover, cross-platform validation on multilingual datasets confirms the framework's transferability, when applied to the review dataset from JD.com.
