AB  - The deployment of large language models (LLMs) on edge hardware presents multilayered challenges, including compression, compiler behavior, and system-level trade-offs. This survey provides a deployment-centric taxonomy of compression strategies for LLMs under edge constraints, bridging algorithmic methods and practical hardware toolchains. We examine quantization techniques, including posttraining and quantization-aware variants, under precision-scaling regimes and analyze how they interact with operator lowering, kernel fusion, and numerical drift across compilers such as tensor virtual machine, core machine learning, and tensor runtime-LLM. Pruning and distillation approaches are reviewed with an emphasis on their impact on generalization, robustness, and hallucination under tight memory and energy budgets. We highlight the limitations of existing benchmarking suites, including the lack of robustness-aware evaluation, numerical fidelity tracking under low-precision inference, and explicit treatment of compiler-induced latency and energy variability. Case studies across real-world deployment stacks illustrate how system-level constraints translate into concrete deployment bottlenecks in practical edge LLM pipelines. Finally, we identify the open challenges related to continual model adaptation at the edge, privacy-preserving personalization, and secure and reproducible deployment under system constraints. This study provides a structured synthesis of techniques and trade-offs relevant to practitioners, compiler engineers, and hardware vendors pursuing sustainable high-performance edge artificial intelligence.
ER  - 

