AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) have recently shown very promising results on medical tasks such as clinical question answering, report summarization, and decision support. However, their high computational cost makes them difficult to use on resource-constrained devices. Quantization reduces model size and enables lightweight deployment, yet the impact of low-bit precision on medical language understanding remains underexplored. In this work, we present a systematic empirical study of low-bit quantization for medical LLMs. We evaluate multiple 2-, 3-, and 4-bit quantization techniques across several medical natural language processing (NLP) benchmarks and report their results on task accuracy, model size, and inference efficiency. Our study focuses on evaluating different quantization methods on medical data rather than device-specific deployment; the findings show that medical LLMs can be compressed without significant performance degradation, making them strong candidates for future edge and mobile healthcare applications.
