AB  - Power utilities increasingly require natural-language interfaces that can explain customer-service rules while recognising fault and safety conditions. This study aims to evaluate whether domain-specific parameter-efficient fine-tuning improves consultation quality and fault-inquiry performance compared with an untuned large language model (LLM) and few-shot prompting. A quantitative, paired experimental design was implemented using a reproducible synthetic corpus of 2,400 power-customer cases distributed across eight consultation and fault categories. After validation, the corpus was divided into 70% training, 15% validation and 15% testing sets, and three conditions were benchmarked: M1 base LLM, M2 few-shot prompting and M3 domain QLoRA. The simulated benchmark showed that M3 achieved 88.89% classification accuracy, a macro F1-score of 0.886 and a BERTScore F1 of 0.893. It also produced 99.07% safety compliance and a 1.39% hallucination rate, while its improvement over M1 was significant under McNemar's test $(\mathrm{p}<0.001)$; blinded expert agreement was strong (weighted kappa $=0.857)$. The results indicate that domain adaptation can materially improve technical relevance and safety-oriented escalation in this task. Utilities should combine QLoRA adaptation with expert-reviewed response policies and continuous monitoring. The proposed pipeline provides a practical basis for customer-service triage and operator decision support, but it should not autonomously control grid assets. The principal limitation is that the reported results are simulation-based and require confirmation using anonymised operational data and a deployed model.
ER  - 

