AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) have shown impressive generalization across many natural language processing tasks. However, the degree to which they can imitate or exceed classical supervised methods for numerical prediction tasks is less established. This paper presents a systematic metric-based evaluation of frontier, open-source and QLoRA fine-tuned LLMS. The aim is to predict price of the products from text-based descriptions from the Amazon Reviews 2023 dataset, and benchmark against traditional supervised regressors. We develop a rigorous experimental pipeline that incorporates data curation, embedding construction, quantization, and ensemble learning. We compare performance across architectures and number of parameters represented. We assess performance using RMSE, R2 and provide resource-cost metrics in the form of inference latency, memory footprint, and quantization-induced degradation. Our results demonstrate that fine-tuned quantized LLMs outperform traditional regressors while offering substantial reductions in computational cost. This study highlights the potential for hybrid architectures in the future in the context of supervised learning.
