AB  - Large language models (LLMs) have rapidly become essential to the development of multimodal AI, enabling the creation of systems capable of understanding and combining information from text, images, audio, and video. Despite their impressive capabilities, these models are often difficult to implement in practice due to their high processing and memory requirements. Knowledge distillation (KD) has emerged as a promising solution: a large teacher model transfers its knowledge to a smaller student model, creating leaner, faster, and more practical systems without loss of accuracy. This article provides a systematic review of recent work on multimodal KD for LLMs, focusing on both the technical methods and their applications. Based on the PRISMA guidelines, 46 studies published between 2024 and 2025 were analyzed, covering approaches such as the teacher-student framework, parametrically efficient methods like low-rank adaptation (LoRA), pre-trained pipelines, and compression strategies including pruning and quantization. This review highlights that KD offers clear efficiency advantages, but challenges remain in areas such as handling noise or data diversity, reliance on synthetic labels, and evaluation practices often limited to accuracy alone. Applications in healthcare, social media analytics, and audiovisual segmentation demonstrate the potential of KD, but also reveal its shortcomings in terms of robustness and reproducibility. By mapping these methods into a structured taxonomy and identifying their limitations, this review opens prospects for the reproducibility, customization, and scalable implementation of multimodal KD with LLM.
ER  - 

