AB  - Multilingual large language models (mLLMs) offer a shared backbone that can support many languages, tasks, and domains. In practice, performance is uneven across languages. Low-resource languages often lag due to limited digital corpora, scarce labeled datasets, under-represented scripts, and restricted compute budgets for training and deployment. These constraints can interact: weak token coverage increases sequence length and can degrade lexical modeling; noisy or misidentified text can dominate small corpora. This paper reviews recent progress on multilingual modeling for low-resource settings and highlights a recurring limitation: many studies improve one component (tokenization, synthetic data, retrieval prompting, or parameter-efficient adaptation) without integrating components into an end-to-end, resourceaware workflow that is reproducible under realistic constraints. To address this, we propose MELM4LR, a modular system blueprint for extending mLLMs to low-resource languages via (i) governance-aware data acquisition and quality control, (ii) scriptand morphology-sensitive tokenizer augmentation with careful embedding initialization, (iii) budgeted continual pretraining with balancing to mitigate language dominance, and (iv) efficient task adaptation using parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) coupled with retrieval and synthetic supervision when labels are scarce. We also define an evaluation protocol that measures task quality alongside robustness, safety checks, and cost, enabling practitioners to report trade-offs that matter in low-resource deployments. Across the reviewed literature, improvements are most reliable when vocabulary coverage, data quality, and adaptation efficiency are addressed jointly rather than in isolation. The paper concludes with open directions on multilingual safety evaluation, equitable benchmarking, and community-centered data practices
ER  - 

