AB  - In recent years, Large Language Models (LLMs) have excelled in different domains to an expert's level. Nevertheless, re-training such models is computationally expensive and incurs additional costs regarding specialized downstream tasks and information that change frequently. To address this problem, end task knowledge is added through common techniques like Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG), Parameter Efficient Fine Tuning (PEFT). This paper critically examines several existing solutions and methodologies to overcome such limitations as computational overhead, hallucination and the end task specialization constraints remains for further study. We also propose a two staged framework, which use Facebook AI Similarity Search (FAISS) with Hierarchical Navigable Small World (HNSW) optimized RAG system and fine tune with LoRA Hyperparameter Tuning under a limited computational setting. In the initial tests, finetuning with the best LoRA hyperparameters on the dataset size and the optimized top 1 context using the optimized RAG system performs very accurate responses, while top 5 context provides accurate and diverse responses with little hallucination. Both training and evaluation were done using a publicly available dataset.
ER  - 

