AB  - The integration of Large Language Models (LLMs) into medical document classification represents a paradigm shift in clinical natural language processing. This comprehensive review examines the current landscape of LLM applications for classifying diverse medical documents including clinical notes, discharge summaries, and radiology reports. We synthesize findings from 2020-2025 literature, categorizing approaches into four primary methodologies: parameter-efficient fine-tuning techniques, prompt-based learning strategies, hybrid multimodal architectures, and retrieval-augmented generation systems. Our analysis demonstrates that LLMs achieve superior performance on complex classification tasks compared to traditional methods, with particular strengths in few-shot learning and contextual understanding. However, significant challenges persist including hallucination mitigation, privacy preservation, computational efficiency, and clinical workflow integration. Emerging trends indicate promising directions in federated learning, human-in-the-loop systems, and lightweight adaptation methods. This review provides a comprehensive framework for researchers and practitioners, highlighting critical gaps and proposing a research agenda focused on clinical validation, ethical considerations, and sustainable deployment strategies in healthcare settings.
