AB  - As novel power systems featuring high penetration of renewable energy and power-electronic devices continue to expand rapidly, the complexity of grid operation risks has increased significantly. Traditional scheduling auxiliary decision methods often struggle with slow computation speeds and inconsistent preplan quality. Although existing $\mathrm{N}-1 / \mathrm{N}-1-1$ contingency analysis algorithms can accurately identify high-risk scenarios through detailed physical modeling, they continue to face the persistent “last-mile” challenge: converting numerical results into executable preplans that dispatchers can directly apply. In this paper, we propose a physical-data dual-driven framework that tightly integrates N-1/N-1-1 fault anticipation at the physical layer with a constrained large language model at the data-driven layer, enabling automatic preplan generation. Our core innovation positions the LLM as a “physically constrained decision expressor” rather than an independent physical-state reasoner. We introduce a Perception-Decision-Execution-Verification-Review (PDEVR) closed-loop framework that formalizes verification and review as independent modules. For domain adaptation, we employ layered LoRA together with curriculum learning and physics-informed augmentation. Additionally, our LLM + RAG dual verification integrates over 200 regulations and $5000+$ historical cases, achieving 99.9% compliance in tested scenarios. Experiments conducted on a real 200 -node provincial grid show that our method delivers $8.75 \times$ faster preplan generation time, 98 % completeness, and 94 % accuracy. Overall, we believe this approach opens a promising new paradigm for intelligent dispatching.
ER  - 

