AB  - Large language models (LLMs) achieve remarkable performance but require significant resource requirements, making them infeasible for latency-critical applications such as human-robot interaction (HRI) and edge computing. Knowledge distillation addresses this gap by transferring capabilities from large teacher models to compact students while preserving performance. This survey reviews knowledge distillation methods for generative LLMs with an emphasis on enabling real-time, embodied, and interactive AI systems. We systematically analyze methods across white-box approaches (reverse KL divergence, cross-vocabulary alignment, layer-wise distillation) and black-box techniques (adversarial learning, context distillation, self-play), integrating complementary compression methods including quantization (GPTQ, AWQ, SmoothQuant), speculative decoding (Medusa, Hydra), and model merging. We identify generativespecific challenges-divergence selection, vocabulary mismatches, error compounding, and self-distillation risks-and analyze deployment trade-offs across cloud, edge, and robotic platforms. Grounded in psycholinguistic evidence that conversational naturalness requires sub- 500 ms response latencies, we validate our analysis through an empirical benchmark of four distilled models (82M-1.1B parameters) on 20 HRI-specific tasks spanning robot commands, social dialogue, grounded reasoning, and safety-critical scenarios. Our results reveal a three-way trade-off between latency, response quality, and safety alignment, with subbillion models achieving the 500ms threshold while larger models fail it on CPU hardware. We conclude with open problems in latency-aware distillation, safety preservation under compression, multimodal grounding for embodied agents, and hardware-aware optimization.
ER  - 

