AB  - Large language models (LLMs) have shown strong potential in mathematical problem solving and have become an important research direction in artificial intelligence. This paper reviews the application of LLMs in mathematical reasoning, focusing on core technologies including prompt engineering, fine-tuning, and symbolic computation integration. Representative optimization strategies such as Chain-of-Thought reasoning, self-consistency, and LoRA adaptation are examined, together with the integration of LLMs and symbolic reasoning systems. The performance of existing approaches is evaluated across different mathematical tasks and benchmark datasets. The review indicates that reasoning strategies, domain-specific training, and external computational tools can significantly improve mathematical problem-solving performance. However, current LLMs still face challenges in complex reasoning, symbolic accuracy, and robustness. Future research should focus on neural-symbolic integration, mathematical knowledge representation, and the development of more accurate, reliable, and interpretable mathematical reasoning systems.
