AB  - The intelligent upgrade of power customer service systems places higher demands on natural language understanding, while general large language models (LLMs) often perform poorly in specific vertical field question answering (Q&A) tasks due to a lack of structured instruction data. To address this issue, this article proposes a large model instruction tuning method called EIPT (Electricity Instruction Prompt Tuning) for power customer service scenarios to improve the model's adaptability and accuracy in industry Q&A tasks. First, we design prompt templates with business logic based on multi-round question-and-answer business processes and typical customer service task corpora, and build a high-quality instruction set through a combination of automatic generation and expert review. Second, we introduce a task distribution balancing mechanism and use small sample clustering and interclass difference analysis to control the coverage of the training data. Finally, we adopt a low-rank adaptation (LoRA) parameter fine-tuning method to enhance model response consistency and inference accuracy while reducing costs. Experimental results on a power customer service question-answering dataset demonstrate that EIPT achieves significant improvements over unoptimized models in terms of answer quality, semantic matching, and execution stability, validating its feasibility and effectiveness in vertical-domain intelligent customer service systems.
