AB  - The exponential growth of Natural Language Processing (NLP) has transformed the analysis of digital feedback, yet a significant disparity remains for low-resource, morphologically rich languages such as Turkish, particularly within domains such as the civil aviation industry. This study addresses the aforementioned gap by establishing a robust and domain-adaptive framework for sentiment analysis (SA) and topic classification using Turkish reviews for the most visited 9 airports in Türkiye. We have constructed a high-quality corpus derived from 8,372 Turkish reviews across major airports in Türkiye, employing proprietary Large Language Models (LLMs), namely GPT (gpt-4o-mini), Gemini (gemini-2.5-flash-lite), and Claude (claude-3-haiku), to generate distinct labels with verified reliability. To mitigate the computational limitations of full model training, we utilize Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (PEFT) strategies, specifically Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA), to adapt 5 open-source base models: BERTurk, XLM-RoBERTa, Llama 3.2, Qwen 2.5, and Gemma 2. We conduct a comprehensive evaluation across 151 fine-tuned model configurations- 93 for SA and 58 for topic classification-to investigate the combined effects of input granularity (review vs. sentence level) alongside assessments of temporal, cross-airport, and out-of-domain generalization. Furthermore, we explore pipeline optimizations through loss function and preprocessing (Raw, Preprocessed and Mixed) strategy ablations, supported by detailed computational cost and qualitative error analyses. The results indicate that the fine-tuned models outperform their zero-shot counterparts, particularly in topic classification, where generic models failed to capture latent themes effectively. While the best zero-shot baseline (orpogemma-2-9b-tr) achieved an F1-Score of only  $0.6037 \pm 0.0108$ , our fine-tuned model (llama_3.2_3b) reached an F1-Score of  $0.8228 \pm 0.0056$  in topic classification, representing an absolute F1-Score improvement of 0.2191. In SA, domain adaptation proved similarly valuable, elevating the base model’s (llama_3.2_3b_base) F1-Score from  $0.5299 \pm 0.0099$  to  $0.9156 \pm 0.0042$  (llama_3.2_3b). Furthermore, our analysis reveals that processing feedback at the review level yields superior predictive performance (achieving an F1-Score of  $0.9156 \pm 0.0042$  by llama_3.2_3b) compared to sentence-level granularity (achieving an F1-Score of  $0.8998 \pm 0.0048$  by llama_3.2_3b) in SA, underscoring the importance of contextual integrity in agglutinative languages. In the out-domain generalization tests on unseen future reviews, llama_3.2_3b reached an F1-Score of 0.9361 in SA and 0.7367 in topic classification.
ER  - 

