AB  - Legal question answering requires accurate, and contextually relevant answers to questions about statutes, regulations and case law. Large language models can produce fluent answers to legal questions but face challenges in understanding complex legal reasoning. So, to enhance the ability to answer legal questions accurately, we have conducted an empirical study of two adaptation methods, fine-tuning and retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), across two architecturally distinct open-source models: the generative Llama 3.2-3B Instruct and the extractive RoBERTa-base-squad2. Four systems (fine-tuned Llama, fine-tuned RoBERTa, Llama RAG and RoBERTa RAG) are implemented on the Open Australian Legal QA dataset. We have evaluated the performance of these systems based on answer quality (Token F1, BERTScore F1, ROUGE, METEOR), grounding (faithfulness and hallucination rate) and retrieval quality. Fine-tuned Llama produced the highest answer quality (Token F1 0.6033, BERTScore F1 0.9444) while the shared hybrid RAG pipeline, combining lexical and dense retrieval with cross-encoder reranking, achieved near-ceiling document recall (Recall@3 0.9887), indicating that the reader rather than retrieval limits end-to-end quality. Generative Llama consistently outperformed extractive RoBERTa under both adaptation methods. All experiments executed on a single 16 GB academic GPU using parameter-efficient techniques.
ER  - 

