AB  - Labelled data scarcity remains a persistent bottleneck in machine learning, particularly when annotation is costly or impractical. Few-shot learning (FSL) addresses this limitation by enabling models to learn fromonly a few examples, analogous to the human ability to generalise from limited exposure. This research aims to find out whether synthetic data generation techniques, namely back-translation and rule-based paraphrasing, can aid few-shot learning of text classification tasks through parameter-efficient fine-tuning using ultralightweight open-source language models. We assessed Microsoft's Phi-2 (2.7B parameters) and Alibaba's Qwen-1.8B with a structured experimental framework using progressively larger subsets of the AG News dataset. The starting point of our assessments is 20 samples (5 per class), which we progressively augment to 40, 60, and 80 samples. The only constant in our experiments is the fixed-sized test set of 200 samples (50 per class). According to the results, both augmentation methods yield a significant performance boost. Phi-2 with backtranslation achieved 84% accuracy at Round 4, an increase of 0.24 from its baseline of 60%, and came within just 2% of its full-data ceiling (86%) using only 20 original samples. Phi-2 back-translation (69%) outperformed its baseline on existing data (63%) at 40 samples, indicating the potential of synthetic data in extremely low-resource settings. Results showed that Qwen-1.8B performed better with paraphrasing, yielding an accuracy of 83%. The best augmentation approach differs by model due to differing pre-training objectives. This research demonstrates that combining synthetic data with parameterefficient fine-tuning of ultra-lightweight models can meaningfully boost few-shot learning performance in low-resource settings.
ER  - 

