AB  - Automatic generation of multiple-choice questions (MCQs) that assess higher-order cognitive skills in Bloom’s taxonomy remains challenging despite advances in large language models (LLMs). This paper presents a systematic study of higher-order MCQ generation using a fixed, literature-validated Chain-of-Thought prompt strategy (PS4) across proprietary and open-source LLMs. Two proprietary models and a parameter-efficient fine-tuned open-source LLaMA model are evaluated under identical prompting conditions. To enable reliable assessment, we propose a Hierarchical Bloom-Aware Evaluation Framework (HBEF) that integrates linguistic validation, pedagogical quality checks, and explicit verification of Analyze-level cognition, supported by both independent LLM-based evaluators and human expert review. Experiments on 260 MCQs show that PS4 reliably enforces linguistic quality and pedagogical validity across models, while higher-order cognitive alignment remains strongly dependent on model capacity. Fine-tuning significantly improves higher-order alignment and distractor quality in the open-source model relative to its base version but does not fully bridge the gap with large proprietary models. The results further reveal evaluator variability and self-evaluation bias in automated assessment, underscoring the need for bias-aware evaluation. Overall, the study highlights the complementary roles of structured prompting, model capacity, fine-tuning, and expert validation in reliable higher-order MCQ generation.
