AB  - Large language models (LLMs) have recently been explored as unified backbones for recommender systems by casting recommendation as a language modeling problem. However, existing LLM-based recommenders largely treat user preference as unstructured text and under-utilize rich aspect-level opinions embedded in reviews. We propose AAPR-Rec, an Aspect-Aware Prompt-Reasoning Recommender that first builds structured aspect–sentiment profiles for users and items and then injects them into LLM prompts via a constrained natural-language template.Across three Amazon datasets, AAPR-Rec improves NDCG@10 by 7.2–9.8% and HR@10 by 6.3–8.7% relative to the best non-aspect LLM baseline, with significance under Holm–Bonferroni correction (α = 0.05). Ablations show that aspect profiles and structured serialization together contribute 4.7–6.9% NDCG@10 gains. Using LoRA on a LLaMA-2 backbone retains 98.9% of full fine-tuning accuracy while achieving a 3.2× inference speedup. We release code, prompts, and preprocessing scripts at https://github.com/RyanLane1/AAPR-Rec for reproducibility.
