AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) demand immense compute and memory resources, exposing the limitations of conventional Central Processing Units (CPUs) and Graphics Processing Units (GPUs). This paper surveys recent advances in Field-Programmable Gate Arrays (FPGAs), Application-Specific Integrated Circuits (ASICs), and memory-centric accelerators, highlighting trade-offs in scalability, efficiency, and deployment flexibility. Emerging solutions such as Serving Low-Rank Adaptation (S-LoRA) and tensor-train (TT) compression demonstrate up to 200× speedups and significant energy savings. Software–hardware co-design and serving frameworks further enhance inference performance. The study consolidates trends, challenges, and optimization gaps crucial for enabling practical and sustainable deployment of next-generation LLMs.
