AB  - Deploying large language models for subjectivity detection in Indonesian ride-hailing reviews presents substantial challenges due to high linguistic variability and security risks in efficiency-constrained environments. Despite growing adoption of parameter-efficient adaptation methods, their robustness under cross-domain and adversarial conditions remains insufficiently examined. This study aims to investigate the trade-off between adversarial robustness and parameter efficiency by comparing Full Fine-Tuning and Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) under a Leave-One-App-Out (LOAO) evaluation framework across Indonesian ride-hailing review datasets from Gojek, Grab, and Maxim. Model resilience is assessed against character-level evasion attacks, including dot insertion and phonetic obfuscation, which exploit the subword tokenization behavior of IndoBERTweet. The key contributions of this study are threefold: (1) empirical evidence that LoRA achieves an average F1-macro of 0.739 while reducing trainable parameters by 97.6%; (2) demonstration that LoRA maintains greater adversarial stability, achieving a robustness gap as low as − 0.79% compared to higher degradation under Full Fine-Tuning; and (3) backdoor resistance analysis showing competitive resilience under training-time compromise scenarios. These findings provide actionable insights into the interactions among linguistic variability, efficiency constraints, and adversarial risk, supporting the deployment of reliable subjectivity-detection systems on Indonesian ride-hailing platforms.
ER  - 

