AB  - Prompt engineering has emerged as a critical paradigm for adapting large-scale foundation models to specialized tasks with minimal parameter updates. This paper presents a systematic crossdomain survey of prompt engineering techniques across Natural Language Processing, Computer Vision, and Software Engineering. By analyzing 70 representative studies from 2022 to 2025, we propose a multi-dimensional taxonomy that categorizes techniques based on their optimization paradigms -Discrete vs. Continuous -and their underlying logic-driven or knowledge-augmented mechanisms. A key contribution of this work is the identification of a significant methodological divergence driven by domain-specific task targets: while NLP focuses on semantic reasoning depth and CV on visual-latent structural alignment, SE necessitates a unique technical route of 'Formal Grounding' through structural artifact injection and RAG-based knowledge integration to ensure deterministic correctness. Furthermore, we document the evolution of evaluation metrics from linguistic similarity (e.g., BLEU) to execution-based functional verification (e.g., Pass@k, CodeBLEU). Finally, we discuss the growing cross-pollination between domains and outline future research directions for building interpretable, knowledge-logic integrated AI assistants for high-stakes technical disciplines.
